  "knowledgePattern": "Derive and reuse a logarithmic input-combination identity",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Work inside the logarithm first",
      "body": "<p>Substitute the suggested combined input into the defining fraction. Put its numerator and denominator over common denominators, then factor.</p>",
      "recap": "Combining compound fractions requires preserving the full numerator and denominator before cancellation.",
      "pitfall": "Every logarithm argument must remain positive.",
      "pause": "Derive how the combined input relates to f(u) and f(v).",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-algebraic-fraction-operations",
          "reminder": "Combining compound fractions requires preserving the full numerator and denominator before cancellation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Reuse the identity with equal inputs",
      "body": "<p>Choose the two inputs equal so that the identity repeats the same function value. Apply that construction as many times as the target multiplier requires.</p>",
      "recap": "A function identity can be used again with new inputs once those inputs satisfy its domain.",
      "pitfall": "Check that the newly constructed input stays in the interval where f is defined.",
      "pause": "Calculate the final input exactly, then check the equation and uniqueness.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l04",
          "reminder": "A function identity can be used again with new inputs once those inputs satisfy its domain."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the denominator is positive on the stated domain. Combining the two fractions gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so w remains in the domain.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Taking logarithms yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the suggested constants are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>, this first gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Applying the same doubling rule to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> gives the input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>12</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>17</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This input lies in the domain since its square is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>288</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>289</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The fraction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> strictly increases on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (cross-multiply its positive denominators for two ordered inputs), and logarithm strictly increases, so f is one-to-one. The solution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></math>, answer D.</p>"
}
